# Agent guide

## What this project is

`dxx` provides enhanced replacements for common Deno commands plus the `xProcess` support library:

- `dxf` — formatter wrapper (`dprint fmt` when available, else `deno fmt`)
- `dxi` — installer that writes an enhanced shim (bash-like command-line/brace/glob expansion on Windows, suppresses the "Terminate batch job (Y/N)?" prompt)
- `dxr` — runner with the same command-line expansion enhancements
- `dxx` — integrator (WIP)
- `xProcess` — library that lets scripts recover their true invocation text and expanded args

Details and usage examples are in [`../README.md`](../README.md). Structure is in [`architecture.mkd`](architecture.mkd).

## Setup

- Install Deno (project targets `std@0.134.0`, so Deno >= 1.20.5). Newer Deno is fine for development.
- No `package.json` / `node_modules`; imports resolve via URL. `deno.lock` is intentionally disabled.
- Optional dev tools (checked for at test time, skipped if absent): `dprint`, `cspell`, `commitlint`, `bmp`, `madge`.
- `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are excluded from all tooling — never edit `vendor/`.

## Workflows

| Task                | Command                                                                           |
| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Run all tests       | `deno test -A`                                                                    |
| Tests, colorized    | `deno task test`                                                                  |
| Lint                | `deno lint`                                                                       |
| Format (write)      | `dprint fmt`                                                                      |
| Format (check)      | `dprint check`                                                                    |
| Style + syntax gate | `deno test -A tests/00.project.style.test.ts tests/00.project.syntax.test.ts`     |
| Coverage            | `deno test -A --coverage=.coverage` then `deno coverage ...` (see `../README.md`) |
| Release testing     | `deno test -A -- --release`                                                       |
| Benchmarks          | `dxr bench/bench-parser.ts`                                                       |

Test flags after `--` (e.g. `--release`, `--test-xxx`) set `TEST_XXX=true` for the run via `setEnvFromArgs`.

## Coding conventions

Formatting is enforced by `dprint` (config in [`../.dprint.json`](../.dprint.json)) — always run `dprint fmt` before committing. Key choices:

- Tabs for indentation, width 2; line width 100
- Single quotes; semicolons; trailing commas only on multi-line
- Imports/exports sorted case-insensitively
- LF newlines, final newline required, no trailing whitespace, no UTF-8 BOM (the style test fails otherwise)

Other norms visible in the codebase:

- Shared/internal modules are prefixed with `$` (e.g. `$deps.ts`, `$shared.ts`, `$mod.ts`)
- Deno standard library is re-exported through `$deps.ts` files rather than imported directly elsewhere
- Add `<!-- spell-checker:ignore ... -->` / `// spell-checker:ignore ...` lines for new proper nouns and jargon so `cspell` passes
- Keep code cross-platform; guard OS-specific behavior with `isWinOS` and platform path helpers

## Commit conventions

Full rules and examples: [`commits.mkd`](commits.mkd). In short: `type[/scope] ~ imperative subject` (note the `~` separator), lowercase type from the `feat` / `fix` / `change` / `refactor` / `perf` / `docs` / `test` / `deps` / `maint` set, header ≤ 90 chars, add a `## why` body section for non-obvious changes. `commitlint` validates every commit as part of the style test.

## Gotchas

- The repo currently has WIP commits and two scratch files (`t-em.ts`, `t-mm.ts`) at root — confirm intent before touching them.
- `deno.lock` is disabled by design; don't re-enable it.
- Console-size / TTY integration tests can't get a real TTY in CI and are handled specially — don't assume failures there are your change.
- `dprint`'s markdown plugin reformats prose to single lines (`textWrap: always`, huge line width); expect that when you run `dprint fmt`.
